We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

- Starting state: #tab-account selected (aria-selected="true"), #tab-notifications disabled (has disabled attribute?), but still present. Actually in frozen journey requirements step 1: they seek #tab-account then press ArrowRight expecting focus moves to #tab-billing and aria-selected becomes true on billing.

But currently, due to disabled notifications tab, pressing ArrowRight from account goes to notifications (since it's next in DOM order) but because it's disabled, the tab JS likely still focuses it? Let's see observed state after ArrowRight: In observed.after, we see the tabs: Account button has aria-selected="false", tabindex="-1". Notifications button has class "nav-link active", aria-selected="true", disabled="" (so disabled attribute present). Billing button remains unselected.

Thus ArrowRight moved focus to the disabled notifications tab, which is still focusable? It seems disabled attribute on button makes it not focusable? But they observed focus moved to body? Wait after state shows focus on body (the whole page). Actually after pressing ArrowRight, focus went to body? Because the disabled button cannot receive focus, so focus maybe went to body? The observed after shows focus selector body (the whole page). So the ArrowRight key event was prevented default? They prevented default in _keydown (event.preventDefault()). Then they compute nextActiveElement via getNextActiveElement, passing isNext true, isCycleAllowed true? Let's check.

In tab.js _keydown: if key is arrow left/right/up/down, they event.stopPropagation(); event.preventDefault(); const isNext = [ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key); const nextActiveElement = getNextActiveElement(this._getChildren(), event.target, isNext, true); Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show();

So they prevent default and stop propagation, then compute next active element using getNextActiveElement from util/index.js. That function takes list, activeElement, shouldGetNext, isCycleAllowed.

Now, this._getChildren() returns SelectorEngine.find(SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM, this._parent). SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM includes .nav-link (not dropdown toggle) etc. For disabled button, does it still match? Yes, the button still matches .nav-link. So it's included in the list.

getNextActiveElement will return the next element in list regardless of disabled status? It does not filter by disabled. It just returns element at index +/-1, with cycling.

Thus from account (index 0), next is notifications (index 1). It returns that element even if disabled. Then they call Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show() on that disabled tab. The show method will attempt to activate it. However, the show method first checks if this._elemIsActive(innerElem) (if already active) returns false. Then it gets active element (currently account). Then it fires hide event on active, show event on target. If not prevented, it calls this._deactivate(active, innerElem) and this._activate(innerElem, active). The _activate adds class active, focuses element, sets aria-selected true, removes tabindex, etc. However, note that the element is disabled (has disabled attribute). The _activate will still set aria-selected true and focus? It calls element.focus() - but if element has disabled attribute, focus() will fail? Actually focusing a disabled button is not possible; focus() will not move focus; the focus remains where it was? In many browsers, calling focus() on a disabled element does nothing and focus stays elsewhere (maybe body). That explains why after the action, focus is on body (since focus didn't move). Also they set aria-selected true on the disabled button (we saw in after state: notifications button has aria-selected="true" and class active). Indeed after state shows notifications button has class "nav-link active", aria-selected="true", disabled attribute present. So the script succeeded in activating the disabled tab (making it appear selected) but focus didn't move because disabled.

Thus the expected behavior: ArrowRight should skip disabled tabs and move to the next enabled tab (billing). To fix, we need to modify getNextActiveElement usage to skip disabled elements, or modify _keydown to find next enabled tab.

Options: Change getNextActiveElement to consider disabled? But that's a util function used elsewhere perhaps. Better to adjust in tab.js _keydown: after getting nextActiveElement, if that element is disabled (via isDisabled), we should keep looking for next enabled element in the direction, respecting wrap.

Simpler: In _keydown, after computing nextActiveElement, we can loop while element is disabled and move to next (using getNextActiveElement again) until we find an enabled element or we've looped all.

We must ensure we don't infinite loop if all tabs disabled (unlikely). We'll implement a safety check.

